WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.387
Local development needs convenience, but it should not depend on production secrets.

2
00:00:05.387 --> 00:00:07.568
Developers may need to run an ASP.

3
00:00:07.568 --> 00:00:13.789
NET Core app, connect to a local database, exercise an integration, or debug a background worker.

4
00:00:13.789 --> 00:00:20.908
Those workflows should use development-scoped credentials and safe sample values rather than production access.

5
00:00:20.908 --> 00:00:22.191
.

6
00:00:22.191 --> 00:00:30.914
NET user secrets are useful for local development, especially when a developer needs values outside source-controlled appsettings files.

7
00:00:30.914 --> 00:00:33.415
They are not a production secret store.

8
00:00:33.415 --> 00:00:39.828
They do not replace a managed secret system, access review, runtime identity, or deployment control.

9
00:00:39.828 --> 00:00:43.677
Treat them as a developer convenience with a narrow purpose.

10
00:00:43.677 --> 00:00:48.615
Local environment variables and local configuration files still require care.

11
00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:55.029
They can leak through shell history, terminal screenshots, scripts, logs, or copied project folders.

12
00:00:55.029 --> 00:01:05.355
Development values should be clearly separated from production values, and documentation should explain where developers are expected to put local-only settings.

13
00:01:05.355 --> 00:01:15.873
Sample projects, demos, training code, and automated tests should use harmless placeholders such as ExampleApiKey, ExampleConnectionString, or ExampleWebhookSecret.

14
00:01:15.873 --> 00:01:20.042
Real production secrets should never be required to run examples.

15
00:01:20.042 --> 00:01:25.108
Test credentials should be scoped to test systems and easy to rotate or revoke.

16
00:01:25.108 --> 00:01:29.213
Developer onboarding and offboarding are part of secret hygiene.

17
00:01:29.213 --> 00:01:36.140
New developers should receive access through approved groups or systems, not through copied secrets in chat.

18
00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:44.798
Departing developers should lose access through identity and group changes rather than forcing teams to hunt for scattered credentials.

19
00:01:44.798 --> 00:01:54.226
Accidental commits can be reduced through ignore files, review habits, secret scanning, pre-commit checks, clear templates, and team documentation.

20
00:01:54.226 --> 00:01:59.421
Prevention works best when the safe path is obvious and the unsafe path is noisy.
